        "icon": "visibility",
        "type": "mindmap",
        "layout": "half_width",
        "color_theme": "yellow",
        "content": {
          "center": "Capsular Phimosis",
          "branches": [
            "Small capsulorexus",
            "Pseudo exfoliation syndrome",
            "Retinitis pigmentosa",
            "Plate haptic silicon IOL"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "title": "Reducing Refractive Surprise",
        "icon": "biometrics",
        "type": "key_point",
        "layout": "half_width",
        "color_theme": "green",
        "content": [
          "Difference in axial length between eyes is <7mm",
          "Difference in average K <0.9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Management of Refractive Surprise",
        "icon": "list",
        "type": "process",
        "layout": "half_width",
        "color_theme": "blue",
        "content": [
          "Step 1: Identify the cause",
          "Step 2: Obtain subjective refraction",
          "Step 3: Exclude extended capsular bag (myopic shift)",
          "Step 4: Exclude CMO (hyperopic shift)",
          "Step 5: Check the biometry and strength of implanted IOL",
          "Step 6: Repeat biometry and keratometry (rule out undiagnosed KC)",
          "Step 7: Measure corneal thickness (rule out undiagnosed previous refractive surgery)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Treatment Options for Refractive Surprise",
        "icon": "medication",
        "type": "key_point",
        "layout": "half_width",
        "color_theme": "purple",
        "content": [
          "Spectacles or contact lenses",
          "IOL exchange (performed early)",
          "Corneal refractive surgery (LASIK/PRK)",
          "Piggy back sulcus IOL implantation"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Pediatric Cataract Surgery Timing",
        "icon": "child_care",
        "type": "remember",
        "layout": "full_width",
        "color_theme": "red",
        "content": {
          "mnemonic": "Critical Windows",
          "explanation": "Bilateral congenital dense cataract: Surgery required between 4 and 10 weeks to prevent stimulus deprivation amblyopia. Unilateral cataract: Surgery should be performed before 6 weeks."
        }
      },
      {
        "title": "Red Flags: Immediate Referral Needed",
        "icon": "emergency",
        "type": "red_flag",
        "layout": "full_width",
        "color_theme": "red",
        "content": [
          "Rapidly progressive pain after cataract surgery",
          "Hypopyon (pus in the anterior chamber)",
          "Loss of red reflex",
          "Decreasing visual acuity in the immediate post-operative period"
        ]
      }
